1
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:09,584
The CTO's question, word for word from the
chapter: "Someone force-pushed with the

2
00:00:09,624 --> 00:00:14,709
'safe' option, --force-with-lease, and a
colleague's commit still disappeared from

3
00:00:14,749 --> 00:00:18,084
the shared branch. How, and
where is that commit now?"

4
00:00:18,424 --> 00:00:23,256
The short answer: the lease was satisfied
by a fetch that nobody looked at, and the

5
00:00:23,296 --> 00:00:27,483
commit still sits in places you can
name. The long answer is this video.

6
00:00:27,523 --> 00:00:32,414
You will build the losing sequence step by
step, watch the guard pass when it should

7
00:00:32,454 --> 00:00:36,230
have refused, and then find the
commit in three different places.

8
00:00:36,270 --> 00:00:37,815
Keep count of those places.

9
00:00:39,655 --> 00:00:43,276
Welcome back to Git and GitHub
Deep Mastery. Pull up a chair.

10
00:00:43,316 --> 00:00:47,598
A push asks the server to move one of
its branches to a commit of yours.

11
00:00:47,638 --> 00:00:52,581
In the last video a push was refused because
it was not a fast-forward, and the fix

12
00:00:52,621 --> 00:00:56,182
was to integrate. But sometimes
you don't want to integrate.

13
00:00:56,222 --> 00:01:01,164
You rebased your own pull request branch.
You squashed two work-in-progress commits

14
00:01:01,204 --> 00:01:05,426
into one. The server's history is the
old one and you want it replaced.

15
00:01:05,766 --> 00:01:10,348
That is what force is for. It is also the
one push that can remove other people's

16
00:01:10,388 --> 00:01:13,828
commits from a branch, including
commits you have never seen.

17
00:01:14,168 --> 00:01:17,919
So today has three layers. Plain
--force and what it destroys.

18
00:01:17,959 --> 00:01:22,627
--force-with-lease, what it compares,
and the exact sequence that defeats it.

19
00:01:22,667 --> 00:01:25,929
And the two stronger forms
that survive that sequence.

20
00:01:25,969 --> 00:01:31,003
Then you cross to the teammate's clone and
see what the rewrite does there, because

21
00:01:31,043 --> 00:01:35,589
the damage has a second act that surprises
people even more than the first.

22
00:01:37,429 --> 00:01:39,007
Here's what you'll walk away with.

23
00:01:39,347 --> 00:01:40,760
After this video you can:

24
00:01:41,100 --> 00:01:45,463
Explain what a forced push does to the
server's ref and to teammates' commits.

25
00:01:45,803 --> 00:01:51,075
Explain what --force-with-lease checks, and
construct the sequence in which it fails

26
00:01:51,115 --> 00:01:51,771
to protect.

27
00:01:52,111 --> 00:01:55,007
Explain what --force-if-includes adds.

28
00:01:55,347 --> 00:01:58,815
Find the overwritten commits
afterwards and say who still has them.

29
00:02:00,655 --> 00:02:05,956
Recall the seven steps of a push. Step 2
was the client rule: an existing branch may

30
00:02:05,996 --> 00:02:11,106
only be fast-forwarded. A forced push tells
step 2 to skip the fast-forward test.

31
00:02:11,146 --> 00:02:16,256
The server's ref is set to your commit,
and whatever it named before is no longer

32
00:02:16,296 --> 00:02:17,782
reachable from that ref.

33
00:02:18,122 --> 00:02:23,159
git push --force 🔴 DANGEROUS. It's also
spelled -f, or a plus sign before one

34
00:02:23,199 --> 00:02:28,631
refspec. This is the first dangerous command
of the video, so the five answers come

35
00:02:28,671 --> 00:02:34,036
before anything is run. Twig looks worried,
and with a red label on screen, fairly

36
00:02:34,076 --> 00:02:34,234
so.

37
00:02:34,574 --> 00:02:38,341
What it changes: the ref on the
server, and your remote-tracking ref.

38
00:02:38,681 --> 00:02:43,303
What it can destroy: every commit on
the server's branch that is not in your

39
00:02:43,343 --> 00:02:48,456
history, including commits you have never
seen. A default bare server has no reflog,

40
00:02:48,496 --> 00:02:50,357
so it keeps no pointer to them.

41
00:02:50,697 --> 00:02:53,969
Try it now. Thirty seconds,
on paper. You have fetched.

42
00:02:54,009 --> 00:02:58,845
Write a git log command that lists the
commits a forced push of your branch would

43
00:02:58,885 --> 00:03:00,471
drop. Then say it out loud.

44
00:03:05,311 --> 00:03:10,450
How to preview, and here's the answer:
git fetch, then git log --oneline

45
00:03:10,490 --> 00:03:15,485
HEAD..origin/<branch> lists exactly
the commits that would be dropped.

46
00:03:15,525 --> 00:03:21,455
git push --dry-run --force prints the
server's current commit ID to the left of the

47
00:03:21,495 --> 00:03:22,246
three dots.

48
00:03:22,586 --> 00:03:27,540
How to recover: the old tip survives in
any clone that had it, a teammate's branch

49
00:03:27,580 --> 00:03:32,351
or the reflog of anybody's remote-tracking
ref, and, until the server's garbage

50
00:03:32,391 --> 00:03:37,467
collection removes it, as an unreachable
object on the server, one that no ref leads

51
00:03:37,507 --> 00:03:39,050
to any more. Push it back.

52
00:03:39,390 --> 00:03:44,541
When it is appropriate: on a branch that
only you use, such as your own pull request

53
00:03:44,581 --> 00:03:48,186
branch after a rebase, or in a
coordinated history rewrite.

54
00:03:48,226 --> 00:03:49,237
Then use a lease.

55
00:03:49,577 --> 00:03:54,838
Now the lease. --force-with-lease means:
force only if the server is where I think

56
00:03:54,878 --> 00:04:00,010
it is. The bare form says "overwrite only
if the server's branch still equals my

57
00:04:00,050 --> 00:04:01,368
remote-tracking ref".

58
00:04:01,708 --> 00:04:06,191
Quick quiz. While you work, your
editor quietly runs git fetch.

59
00:04:06,231 --> 00:04:10,068
Does your lease, A, stay as
it was, or B, get renewed?

60
00:04:10,108 --> 00:04:10,929
Your answer?

61
00:04:15,769 --> 00:04:18,641
It's B, and there is the
weakness, in the definition.

62
00:04:18,681 --> 00:04:22,047
The lease does not compare the
server with what you have seen.

63
00:04:22,087 --> 00:04:24,959
It compares the server with
your remote-tracking ref.

64
00:04:24,999 --> 00:04:29,464
Anything that moves that ref renews the
lease: an editor's auto-fetch, a scheduled

65
00:04:29,504 --> 00:04:31,222
job, or you in another terminal.

66
00:04:31,562 --> 00:04:36,918
--force-if-includes adds a second condition:
the tip of the remote-tracking ref must

67
00:04:36,958 --> 00:04:42,314
be reachable from some entry in the reflog
of your local branch, meaning your branch

68
00:04:42,354 --> 00:04:47,582
contained that commit at some time. The
setting push.useForceIfIncludes=true turns

69
00:04:47,622 --> 00:04:48,995
it on for every lease.

70
00:04:49,335 --> 00:04:53,902
And the explicit form,
--force-with-lease=<ref>:<expect>, ignores the

71
00:04:53,942 --> 00:04:58,376
remote-tracking ref and compares
the server with a commit you name.

72
00:04:58,716 --> 00:05:03,910
When not to force at all: on a branch that
someone else pushes to, unless you have

73
00:05:03,950 --> 00:05:09,209
told them. And never as a routine undo on
main. The textbook marks that as outdated

74
00:05:09,249 --> 00:05:14,252
advice: tutorials that teach "git reset
--hard, then git push -f" teach the one

75
00:05:14,292 --> 00:05:17,636
habit in this chapter that
loses other people's work.

76
00:05:17,676 --> 00:05:20,125
Undo published commits with git revert.

77
00:05:21,965 --> 00:05:26,489
A picture helps. The textbook's model
for the lease is the compare-and-swap of

78
00:05:26,529 --> 00:05:31,228
optimistic locking: "set the branch to my
commit only if it still has the value I

79
00:05:31,268 --> 00:05:31,872
last read".

80
00:05:32,212 --> 00:05:36,554
It breaks at the word "I". Your Git does
the reading, and a fetch that you never

81
00:05:36,594 --> 00:05:41,156
looked at refreshes the value. In a database,
you read a row, you compute, you write

82
00:05:41,196 --> 00:05:45,703
back with the old value as a condition.
Here, something else can re-read the row on

83
00:05:45,743 --> 00:05:50,140
your behalf, silently, between your computing
and your writing, and the condition

84
00:05:50,180 --> 00:05:52,222
then holds for a value you never used.

85
00:05:52,562 --> 00:05:57,772
So keep two things apart that feel like
one: what my clone has fetched, and what I

86
00:05:57,812 --> 00:06:01,934
have seen or integrated. A
remote-tracking ref records the first.

87
00:06:01,974 --> 00:06:06,735
Only your branch's own history records
the second, and that is exactly what

88
00:06:06,775 --> 00:06:11,921
--force-if-includes consults. This is
today's case, a little ahead of the demo: a

89
00:06:11,961 --> 00:06:17,171
fetch has put origin/feature/prompt-cache
on 833bc8a, and your branch, at 1a1fa49,

90
00:06:17,211 --> 00:06:19,220
has never contained that commit.

91
00:06:21,060 --> 00:06:25,439
Row 3 is the lease working. Row 4
is nobody doing anything wrong.

92
00:06:25,479 --> 00:06:29,518
Row 5 is the same command as
row 3 with the opposite result.

93
00:06:31,358 --> 00:06:36,416
Step 1: the rewrite. You squash two published
commits into one, after marking the

94
00:06:36,456 --> 00:06:37,800
old tip with a branch.

95
00:06:44,940 --> 00:06:49,341
The graph shows your new commit
1a1fa49 beside the old pair, with

96
00:06:49,381 --> 00:06:53,576
backup/prompt-cache on the old
tip. Status: ahead 1, behind 2.

97
00:06:53,616 --> 00:06:57,197
That is what a rewrite looks
like to Git: divergence.

98
00:06:57,537 --> 00:07:03,259
Step 2: the lease holds. Meanwhile Asha
pushes a test commit on top of the old

99
00:07:03,299 --> 00:07:07,249
history. You try to publish.
Predict. Say it out loud.

100
00:07:07,289 --> 00:07:07,987
I'll wait.

101
00:07:12,827 --> 00:07:19,072
! [rejected] ... (stale info). Your
remote-tracking ref says 12cdd3d, and the server

102
00:07:19,112 --> 00:07:25,357
has the commit that starts 833bc8a. The word
is rejected, without "remote": your own

103
00:07:25,397 --> 00:07:29,771
Git decided, from the advertisement.
Asha's commit is safe.

104
00:07:30,111 --> 00:07:32,009
Step 3: something fetches.

105
00:07:36,849 --> 00:07:41,051
Your remote-tracking ref now equals the
server. You have not looked at Asha's

106
00:07:41,091 --> 00:07:43,200
commit, but the lease cannot know that.

107
00:07:43,540 --> 00:07:47,819
Step 4: the two stronger forms.
Predict whether each of these passes.

108
00:07:52,659 --> 00:07:57,609
Both refuse. With --force-if-includes:
"remote ref updated since checkout".

109
00:07:57,649 --> 00:08:03,132
Your branch never contained 833bc8a. With
the explicit form, the server is compared

110
00:08:03,172 --> 00:08:08,256
with the backup branch at the commit you
rewrote, and it differs: stale info.

111
00:08:08,596 --> 00:08:13,413
Step 5: the bare lease. Same
command as step 2. Predict.

112
00:08:18,253 --> 00:08:23,979
+ 833bc8a...1a1fa49 (forced
update), exit status 0.

113
00:08:24,319 --> 00:08:29,785
Asha's commit is no longer on the server's
branch. And in your clone, no branch

114
00:08:29,825 --> 00:08:31,527
reaches 833bc8a any more.

115
00:08:31,867 --> 00:08:34,052
Step 6: where is the commit now?

116
00:08:34,392 --> 00:08:38,440
Place one: your own clone. The
background fetch recorded it, and

117
00:08:38,480 --> 00:08:43,678
origin/feature/prompt-cache@{1} names it.
Place two: the server's object database,

118
00:08:43,718 --> 00:08:48,853
as an unreachable commit. The server has
no logs directory at all, so no pointer,

119
00:08:48,893 --> 00:08:49,875
only the object.

120
00:08:50,215 --> 00:08:53,686
Step 7: the teammate's
clone. Asha fetches.

121
00:08:54,026 --> 00:08:57,339
Place three: Asha's local
branch still has 833bc8a.

122
00:08:57,379 --> 00:09:02,797
And learn this line: a plus sign, three
dots, and "(forced update)" in fetch output

123
00:09:02,837 --> 00:09:06,216
is the signal that a branch
was rewritten under you.

124
00:09:06,556 --> 00:09:10,783
She is ahead 3, behind 1. The
reflex is git pull --rebase.

125
00:09:10,823 --> 00:09:14,608
Predict what happens to her
commit "Add cache test".

126
00:09:19,448 --> 00:09:24,279
"Successfully rebased", no conflict, and
her commit is no longer on the branch.

127
00:09:24,319 --> 00:09:29,088
Look at the middle command: git merge-base
--fork-point returns her own pushed

128
00:09:29,128 --> 00:09:29,519
commit.

129
00:09:29,859 --> 00:09:35,012
Pull with rebase replays only the commits
after the fork point, and the fork point

130
00:09:35,052 --> 00:09:38,495
is found through the reflog
of the remote-tracking ref.

131
00:09:38,535 --> 00:09:43,814
So nothing was left to replay. If that feels
unfair to Asha, hold on: the root-cause

132
00:09:43,854 --> 00:09:45,398
box says why Git does it.

133
00:09:45,738 --> 00:09:49,874
Step 8: recovery. git cherry-pick
🟡 CAUTION, from her reflog.

134
00:09:57,414 --> 00:10:02,993
HEAD@{2} is "commit: Add cache test". She
cherry-picks it onto the new tip, which

135
00:10:03,033 --> 00:10:08,611
makes a new commit with the same change.
It becomes 9074b47, and an ordinary push

136
00:10:08,651 --> 00:10:09,513
publishes it.

137
00:10:09,853 --> 00:10:15,753
The other forms. labs/run ch12/lease-forms.
Two branches were reworded locally.

138
00:10:15,793 --> 00:10:20,040
Asha has pushed to docs/runbook
and you have not fetched.

139
00:10:20,380 --> 00:10:23,803
--force-with-lease=<ref>
leases and forces one ref.

140
00:10:23,843 --> 00:10:29,099
Every other ref in the same push follows
the normal rules, so docs/runbook was

141
00:10:29,139 --> 00:10:34,531
rejected. The push was also not atomic:
one ref was overwritten and one refused.

142
00:10:34,871 --> 00:10:39,841
An empty expected value means "the ref
must not exist yet": a create-only push.

143
00:10:39,881 --> 00:10:44,724
Without the lease, the second command
would have been a legal fast-forward of

144
00:10:44,764 --> 00:10:45,485
release/1.0.

145
00:10:45,825 --> 00:10:50,408
Now plain force, on docs/runbook, where
you have never fetched Asha's commit.

146
00:10:50,748 --> 00:10:56,701
Your clone believes the server is at 17783a7.
The dry run prints 73f4877...ca3cabb:

147
00:10:56,741 --> 00:11:02,694
the server is at 73f4877, and git cat-file
says your repository has no such object.

148
00:11:02,734 --> 00:11:08,037
That is Asha's commit. The forced push
replaces it, and the reflog of your

149
00:11:08,077 --> 00:11:12,224
remote-tracking ref goes from
17783a7 straight to ca3cabb.

150
00:11:12,264 --> 00:11:15,546
Your clone never held
the commit it destroyed.

151
00:11:15,886 --> 00:11:19,372
It survives in Asha's clone and,
unreachable, on the server.

152
00:11:19,412 --> 00:11:23,839
The other two unreachable commits are
your own earlier versions, replaced on

153
00:11:23,879 --> 00:11:24,310
purpose.

154
00:11:24,650 --> 00:11:29,897
Under protocol version 2, the default, a
plain Git server hands out any object it

155
00:11:29,937 --> 00:11:34,989
has when asked by full ID. An abbreviation
is not accepted, and under protocol

156
00:11:35,029 --> 00:11:40,472
version 0 the request is refused. This is
a recovery route for as long as the object

157
00:11:40,512 --> 00:11:45,955
exists. It is also one reason why a forced
push does not remove a leaked secret from

158
00:11:45,995 --> 00:11:46,543
a server.

159
00:11:48,383 --> 00:11:50,031
Five mistakes to watch for.

160
00:11:50,371 --> 00:11:55,425
Treating --force-with-lease as "safe force".
Root cause: the bare lease compares the

161
00:11:55,465 --> 00:12:00,459
server with your remote-tracking ref, and
any fetch, including one you never looked

162
00:12:00,499 --> 00:12:01,308
at, renews it.

163
00:12:01,648 --> 00:12:04,898
Using plain --force on a
branch you have not fetched.

164
00:12:04,938 --> 00:12:10,050
Root cause: nothing is checked, so commits
you have never seen are dropped and your

165
00:12:10,090 --> 00:12:11,539
own clone holds no copy.

166
00:12:11,879 --> 00:12:15,348
Running git pull --rebase after
a "(forced update)" line.

167
00:12:15,388 --> 00:12:20,211
Root cause: the fork point comes from the
reflog of the remote-tracking ref, so

168
00:12:20,251 --> 00:12:25,382
commits you had already pushed are treated
as removed upstream and are not replayed.

169
00:12:25,722 --> 00:12:29,139
Believing a forced push removes
a commit from the server.

170
00:12:29,179 --> 00:12:34,172
Root cause: the old commits remain as
unreachable objects until garbage collection,

171
00:12:34,212 --> 00:12:36,719
and a server may hand them out by full ID.

172
00:12:37,059 --> 00:12:40,826
Looking for the overwritten
commit in the server's reflog.

173
00:12:40,866 --> 00:12:46,340
Root cause: core.logAllRefUpdates defaults
to false in a bare repository, so a plain

174
00:12:46,380 --> 00:12:47,718
bare server has none.

175
00:12:49,558 --> 00:12:55,295
Now, out of the lab. The textbook gives
a routine for rewriting a branch that is

176
00:12:55,335 --> 00:12:59,194
already published. One: fetch
and read git status -sb.

177
00:12:59,234 --> 00:13:03,815
Two: mark the old tip, git branch
backup/<name>. Three: rewrite.

178
00:13:03,855 --> 00:13:07,859
Four: push with
--force-with-lease=<name>:backup/<name>.

179
00:13:07,899 --> 00:13:11,830
Five: tell everyone who has
the branch what to do next.

180
00:13:11,870 --> 00:13:17,535
And set push.useForceIfIncludes=true once,
for the days you forget the explicit

181
00:13:17,575 --> 00:13:17,896
form.

182
00:13:18,236 --> 00:13:23,521
Picture an evaluation team where two people
share a long-running experiment branch.

183
00:13:23,561 --> 00:13:28,461
One of them rebases it on Friday evening
using that routine, minus step five.

184
00:13:28,501 --> 00:13:32,054
On Monday the other runs git
pull --rebase out of habit.

185
00:13:32,094 --> 00:13:37,123
It says "Successfully rebased". Their
Friday commit is gone from the branch and

186
00:13:37,163 --> 00:13:42,448
nothing was red. Step five is not politeness.
It is the only control for the second

187
00:13:42,488 --> 00:13:43,411
root-cause box.

188
00:13:43,751 --> 00:13:48,498
On GitHub, according to the documentation,
"Block force pushes" is enabled by

189
00:13:48,538 --> 00:13:53,597
default in a new ruleset, and classic branch
protection rules disable force pushes

190
00:13:53,637 --> 00:13:58,757
by default. GitHub offers no reflog that
Git can query, but its Activity view lists

191
00:13:58,797 --> 00:14:03,794
force pushes and offers a comparison for
each entry, and its troubleshooting page

192
00:14:03,834 --> 00:14:08,892
gives the plain-Git answer: ask a collaborator
who still has the commit to push it

193
00:14:08,932 --> 00:14:13,556
to a new branch. The interface changes.
The documentation is the reference.

194
00:14:15,396 --> 00:14:22,903
Your turn. Do Lab 7.4, "When --force-with-lease
saves you and when it does not", in

195
00:14:22,943 --> 00:14:25,267
lab-manual/m07-remotes.md.

196
00:14:25,607 --> 00:14:28,365
Before each forced push
in the lab, write down:

197
00:14:28,705 --> 00:14:32,519
The value of your remote-tracking ref,
and the value you believe the server has.

198
00:14:32,859 --> 00:14:35,943
Whether your local branch has
ever contained the server's tip.

199
00:14:36,283 --> 00:14:41,831
For each of the three forms, bare lease,
lease plus --force-if-includes, explicit

200
00:14:41,871 --> 00:14:45,280
lease: accepted or rejected,
and with which words.

201
00:14:45,620 --> 00:14:49,857
The challenge is Incident 2,
incidents/02-force-push-wrong-branch.

202
00:14:49,897 --> 00:14:53,551
Read only the symptoms file
and work from the repository.

203
00:14:55,391 --> 00:14:59,461
Q203: "Why does --force-with-lease exist?"

204
00:15:01,801 --> 00:15:06,579
Answer out loud first. A strong answer
starts from the problem, what plain force

205
00:15:06,619 --> 00:15:10,976
does not check, and states the lease
as a condition on two specific refs.

206
00:15:11,016 --> 00:15:15,673
It does not stop there: it names the
assumption the condition rests on and one

207
00:15:15,713 --> 00:15:20,310
ordinary event that breaks it. It then
gives the stronger forms and what each

208
00:15:20,350 --> 00:15:25,128
compares instead. A senior answer finishes
with team practice: on which branches

209
00:15:25,168 --> 00:15:29,705
forcing is acceptable at all, and what
a server can be configured to refuse.

210
00:15:31,545 --> 00:15:34,473
Let's land this. You
should now be able to say:

211
00:15:34,813 --> 00:15:39,800
A forced push skips the fast-forward test
and sets the server's ref to my commit.

212
00:15:39,840 --> 00:15:44,952
Every commit that only the old tip reached
becomes unreachable there, and a default

213
00:15:44,992 --> 00:15:46,690
bare server keeps no reflog.

214
00:15:47,030 --> 00:15:52,052
The bare lease compares the server with my
remote-tracking ref, so a fetch I never

215
00:15:52,092 --> 00:15:57,237
looked at defeats it. --force-if-includes
also requires that the remote tip was once

216
00:15:57,277 --> 00:16:01,126
in my branch, and the explicit
lease names the expected commit.

217
00:16:01,166 --> 00:16:06,249
Overwritten commits survive in teammates'
clones, in the reflogs of remote-tracking

218
00:16:06,289 --> 00:16:09,150
refs, and as unreachable
objects on the server.

219
00:16:09,490 --> 00:16:14,863
After a "(forced update)" line I look before
I integrate, because pull --rebase can

220
00:16:14,903 --> 00:16:17,603
drop my pushed commits without a conflict.

221
00:16:19,443 --> 00:16:22,162
Read section 12.8 of Chapter 12.

222
00:16:22,502 --> 00:16:26,899
Then close the book and write the lease-defeating
sequence from memory as a timeline

223
00:16:26,939 --> 00:16:29,539
with three columns: you,
the server, the teammate.

224
00:16:29,879 --> 00:16:34,956
Today you answered the CTO: the lease was
renewed by a fetch nobody read, and the

225
00:16:34,996 --> 00:16:40,073
lost commit was in three places you can
now name. Do Lab 7.4 while this is fresh.

226
00:16:40,113 --> 00:16:45,001
Next time: more than one remote, pruning,
and branches whose upstream is gone.

227
00:16:45,041 --> 00:16:48,286
Until then, look at the
state first and type second.

228
00:16:48,326 --> 00:16:49,802
See you in the next one.
